  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and the 12Z upper air analysis keep\nthe area under broad quasi-zonal flow along the southern\nperiphery of the Westerlies and broad cyclonic flow regime\nacross the northern CONUS into Canada, meanwhile the western\nCONUS anticyclone still anchored near the Four Corners. A\nslow-moving, modified frontal zone remains draped from south\ncentral Kansas into the Ozarks and continues to serve as the\nfocus for convection. Embedded perturbations rounding the base\nof the broader cyclonic flow provide the only meaningful forcing\nfor ascent, keeping the setup marginal but not negligible.\n\nAttention turns to the overnight period as the LLJ strengthens\ngenerally after 03Z and isentropic ascent increases along its\nnose. Forecast soundings across east-central Kansas show MUCAPE\nnear 2400 J/kg with CIN around 240, which points to elevated\nstorms the likely outcome with the surface layer remaining\ncapped. Hodograph structures tend to be somewhat elongated as\nthe LLJ ramps up which could become supportive of rotating\nupdrafts which may help storms persist allowing for at least\nquarter size hail as the primary threat. Gusty winds remain\npossible with the stronger cores but the elevated nature of the\nstorms should limit any widespread wind threat.\n\nThe greater concern tonight focuses on locally heavy rainfall\nmainly across east-central Kansas. If the 850-300mb mean flow\nremains around 15-20kts with the moisture axis overlapping the\narea as the LLJ sets up then any training over the previously\nsaturated areas would likely raise the flooding risk quickly.\nNBM guidance carries the highest QPF across some of the\neast-central areas into the overnight period around midnight\ninto the early morning hours so these areas bear watching\nthrough the overnight hours.\n\nAny lingering light rain exits Friday afternoon as the mid-level\nridge expands eastward into the Central Plains. Temperatures\nclimb steadily on Saturday, with highs in the low to mid 90s and\ndewpoints holding in the low 70s pushing heat indices near 100\ndegrees Saturday and peaking Sunday into Monday around 105 each\nday. Air temperatures reaching triple digits appear unlikely\nbased on current trends and datasets, so the heat index remains\nthe primary concern driven by the low level moisture return into\nthe region and local impacts from the microclimate set up by\nagriculture production still in swing. Uncertainty in the warm\nforecast comes in the form of the northeastern flank of the\nridge overall continuing to flatten with northwest flow\ndisplaced just north of the area. Subtle shortwaves within that\ncorridor could produce enough ascent for either cloud cover to\nbe higher than forecast or overnight precipitation to inhibit\nafternoon heating. The ensemble spreads through the weekend\nremain wide enough that headlines are premature at publish time\nfor this forecast cycle. Ensembles favor the ridge flattening\nfurther by midweek, with a surface front and embedded shortwaves\nreturning better precipitation chances to the area so overall\nuncertainty in the long range forecast is relatively high.",
